WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.481 --> 00:00:02.914
In the previous video, we asked how to

2
00:00:02.994 --> 00:00:06.081
compare nitrate concentrations
across several catchments.

3
00:00:06.161 --> 00:00:08.544
We want to visualise a distribution.

4
00:00:08.624 --> 00:00:11.711
We could calculate a mean
for each catchment,

5
00:00:11.791 --> 00:00:15.431
then show those means in a bar chart.

6
00:00:15.511 --> 00:00:17.894
But two groups can have

7
00:00:17.974 --> 00:00:20.508
the same mean and still be very different.

8
00:00:20.588 --> 00:00:23.323
That is the case here with groups A and B.

9
00:00:23.403 --> 00:00:25.535
Both have a mean of 3,

10
00:00:25.615 --> 00:00:27.877
but their spread is very different.

11
00:00:27.969 --> 00:00:30.969
An unusual value can also
shift the mean considerably,

12
00:00:31.049 --> 00:00:34.247
even if all the other
observations are identical.

13
00:00:34.327 --> 00:00:38.122
Here, the means differ greatly.
Yet only one point

14
00:00:38.202 --> 00:00:42.046
in group B lies further
out than in group A.

15
00:00:42.126 --> 00:00:44.480
So we will build

16
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.014
a visual summary of each distribution.

17
00:00:47.094 --> 00:00:49.925
Let us start with the values
from one catchment

18
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:52.063
and put them in order.

19
00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:56.583
Remember the median from session 2.

20
00:00:56.663 --> 00:01:01.070
It divides the ordered
series into two equal halves.

21
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.448
Here, we have an odd number
of measurements. We leave seven values

22
00:01:05.528 --> 00:01:09.935
on each side.
The central value is our median.

23
00:01:10.015 --> 00:01:13.159
But the median does not tell us

24
00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:16.647
whether the values are close
together or spread out.

25
00:01:16.727 --> 00:01:19.806
To find out, let us look at the interval

26
00:01:19.886 --> 00:01:23.571
spanned by the central 50% of values.

27
00:01:23.663 --> 00:01:27.955
We want a value that roughly separates

28
00:01:28.035 --> 00:01:32.686
the first quarter of observations
from the remaining three quarters.

29
00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:36.998
Take the values to the left
of the overall median

30
00:01:37.078 --> 00:01:41.370
and calculate their own median.

31
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.523
There are seven values on the left.

32
00:01:45.615 --> 00:01:48.761
We set aside the first three and the last

33
00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:52.574
three. The central value,

34
00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:56.329
2.4 here, is the first quartile,
denoted Q1.

35
00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:00.787
We do the same on the right:
with three values

36
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:05.560
on either side, the central
value is the third quartile, Q3.

37
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.237
One clarification:
depending on the calculation

38
00:02:09.317 --> 00:02:12.344
method and the sample size,

39
00:02:12.424 --> 00:02:15.901
two software packages may give
slightly different quartiles.

40
00:02:15.981 --> 00:02:19.459
This is not necessarily an error, but it

41
00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:23.016
needs to be considered.
The key is to use a consistent method,

42
00:02:23.096 --> 00:02:26.704
the same one throughout the analysis.

43
00:02:26.796 --> 00:02:28.306
So do not change methods

44
00:02:28.386 --> 00:02:30.014
halfway through a study.

45
00:02:30.476 --> 00:02:34.008
Between Q1 and Q3 lies the central half

46
00:02:34.088 --> 00:02:37.730
of the observations: the middle 50%.

47
00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.686
This is what the small box represents.

48
00:02:40.766 --> 00:02:43.478
The line in the middle marks the median.

49
00:02:43.558 --> 00:02:47.692
The two edges of the box
correspond to Q1 and Q3.

50
00:02:47.772 --> 00:02:50.867
We have just built the core

51
00:02:50.947 --> 00:02:55.216
of the box-and-whisker plot,

52
00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:58.664
or boxplot,
mentioned in the previous video.

53
00:02:58.956 --> 00:03:03.491
The distance between Q1 and Q3
is the interquartile range,

54
00:03:03.571 --> 00:03:08.461
usually abbreviated to IQR.

55
00:03:08.541 --> 00:03:12.579
We calculate it by subtracting Q1

56
00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:16.910
from Q3. Here, we get 1.1 mg/L: Q1 is 2.4

57
00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:21.392
and Q3 is 3.5,
giving a difference of 1.1 mg/L.

58
00:03:23.084 --> 00:03:26.319
This small box now represents our data.

59
00:03:26.399 --> 00:03:29.288
But if we look at the entire series,

60
00:03:29.368 --> 00:03:32.257
some values lie outside the box.

61
00:03:32.337 --> 00:03:35.077
How can we represent these values,

62
00:03:35.157 --> 00:03:37.848
which are not part

63
00:03:37.928 --> 00:03:40.817
of the central 50%?

64
00:03:40.897 --> 00:03:42.994
We can see two situations here.

65
00:03:43.074 --> 00:03:45.962
Some points are fairly close to the box.

66
00:03:46.042 --> 00:03:48.368
There are three on the left
and two on the right.

67
00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.359
Another point is much further away.

68
00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:55.320
One advantage of Q1,

69
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.306
Q3, the median and the interquartile

70
00:03:59.386 --> 00:04:03.712
range is that they are relatively
insensitive to a very distant value.

71
00:04:03.792 --> 00:04:06.544
This summary depends
on how many values lie

72
00:04:06.624 --> 00:04:09.429
outside the box,
but little on how far away they are.

73
00:04:09.509 --> 00:04:13.181
For example, the value just above 7

74
00:04:13.273 --> 00:04:16.130
could have been 7, 6 or 5

75
00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:19.209
without changing the shape of the box.

76
00:04:19.673 --> 00:04:22.338
To represent values

77
00:04:22.418 --> 00:04:25.877
further out, we use whiskers.

78
00:04:25.957 --> 00:04:29.903
A common convention is to apply the rule

79
00:04:29.983 --> 00:04:34.234
of 1.5 times the interquartile range,
or 1.5 IQR.

80
00:04:34.314 --> 00:04:36.736
In our case,

81
00:04:36.816 --> 00:04:39.805
the interquartile range is 1.1 mg/L.

82
00:04:40.057 --> 00:04:43.898
Multiplying it by 1.5 gives 1.65.

83
00:04:43.978 --> 00:04:48.682
First,
we calculate the lower and upper fences.

84
00:04:48.762 --> 00:04:53.466
For the lower fence,
we subtract 1.65 from Q1.

85
00:04:53.546 --> 00:04:58.249
For the upper fence, we add 1.65 to Q3.

86
00:04:58.329 --> 00:05:01.543
This gives us fences of

87
00:05:01.623 --> 00:05:05.309
0.75 and 5.15 in this example.

88
00:05:06.553 --> 00:05:08.517
Be careful: the whiskers

89
00:05:08.597 --> 00:05:11.168
do not necessarily extend
all the way to these fences.

90
00:05:11.248 --> 00:05:13.985
They stop at the most extreme observations

91
00:05:14.065 --> 00:05:17.967
that still fall within the fences.

92
00:05:18.047 --> 00:05:22.005
Here, the highest value lies

93
00:05:22.085 --> 00:05:24.375
outside the fences, so we do not

94
00:05:24.455 --> 00:05:26.744
use it to position the whiskers.

95
00:05:26.824 --> 00:05:29.917
Instead, among the observations

96
00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:33.953
between 0.75 and 5.15,

97
00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:38.350
we use the two most extreme
ones to position the whiskers.

98
00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:43.716
Values outside the fences
therefore do not determine

99
00:05:43.796 --> 00:05:49.981
the whisker ends,
which are 1.8 and 4 here.

100
00:05:50.105 --> 00:05:54.563
Our boxplot is now complete.

101
00:05:54.643 --> 00:05:58.922
We can easily see the median

102
00:05:59.002 --> 00:06:03.580
and where the central
half of the data lies.

103
00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:08.238
We can also quickly spot
observations worth checking.

104
00:06:08.318 --> 00:06:11.005
Here, the value 7.1 stands out.

105
00:06:11.097 --> 00:06:14.478
Is it an error

106
00:06:14.558 --> 00:06:17.994
or an interesting observation?

107
00:06:18.074 --> 00:06:21.887
We need to revisit the protocol
and the data before

108
00:06:21.967 --> 00:06:25.645
deciding whether to remove
or keep this value.

109
00:06:26.145 --> 00:06:29.851
Remember: the boxplot's central summary is

110
00:06:29.931 --> 00:06:33.911
relatively insensitive
to how high the value 7.1 is.

111
00:06:33.991 --> 00:06:37.996
The same would not be true
of the mean or standard deviation.

112
00:06:38.473 --> 00:06:41.091
This slide summarises those questions.

113
00:06:41.171 --> 00:06:43.289
Only one value appears unusual.

114
00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:47.136
Is it a recording error
or a faulty instrument?

115
00:06:47.216 --> 00:06:50.084
Or could it be the most
interesting event of all?

116
00:06:50.164 --> 00:06:52.782
We need to revisit
the protocol to find out.

117
00:06:52.862 --> 00:06:54.331
Also consider the sample size.

118
00:06:54.411 --> 00:06:58.568
Here,
the dataset contains few observations.

119
00:06:58.648 --> 00:07:00.070
There are only fifteen: n = 15.

120
00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:04.039
Removing this value can therefore
shift the mean considerably.

121
00:07:04.119 --> 00:07:07.900
We need to examine
the consequences of removing it,

122
00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:11.949
including changes
in the statistical results.

123
00:07:13.353 --> 00:07:16.464
The graph alone cannot
settle the question.

124
00:07:16.544 --> 00:07:20.197
We need to revisit the data's origin,
the table and the protocol.

125
00:07:20.841 --> 00:07:23.950
We can now draw a boxplot
for each catchment,

126
00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:27.829
keeping the same scale for comparison.

127
00:07:27.909 --> 00:07:33.071
We can compare the medians,
Q1 and Q3 directly.

128
00:07:33.151 --> 00:07:36.313
The South catchment has a higher median

129
00:07:36.393 --> 00:07:38.896
than the others and a box

130
00:07:38.976 --> 00:07:40.877
spanning a slightly wider interval.

131
00:07:41.097 --> 00:07:44.267
The whiskers also show
how far the values extend

132
00:07:44.347 --> 00:07:47.915
and help us spot possible asymmetry.

133
00:07:47.995 --> 00:07:52.248
At South, the box extends
a little further towards

134
00:07:52.328 --> 00:07:56.638
higher values:
the distance from Q1 to the median

135
00:07:56.718 --> 00:08:01.165
is smaller than the distance
from the median to Q3.

136
00:08:01.257 --> 00:08:04.892
Putting the boxes on the same axis

137
00:08:04.972 --> 00:08:08.911
makes the comparison clearer
here than using points alone.

138
00:08:08.991 --> 00:08:12.931
We can add the points to show

139
00:08:13.011 --> 00:08:16.950
everything explicitly, but be careful:

140
00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:21.293
too many elements can
make the graph unreadable.

141
00:08:22.953 --> 00:08:26.611
A boxplot is not a magic solution.

142
00:08:26.691 --> 00:08:29.807
It combines several pieces of information,

143
00:08:29.887 --> 00:08:34.140
but it can also hide some of them.

144
00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:37.011
Consider these two
apparently identical boxes

145
00:08:37.091 --> 00:08:40.207
for series A and B.

146
00:08:40.287 --> 00:08:44.645
The problem is that the sample
size for each series

147
00:08:44.725 --> 00:08:46.029
is not shown by the boxplot.

148
00:08:46.593 --> 00:08:50.704
We therefore need to display
the sample size, n,

149
00:08:50.784 --> 00:08:55.245
or show the points when necessary.

150
00:08:55.325 --> 00:08:57.631
This allows us to compare the two series.

151
00:08:57.711 --> 00:09:02.056
Here, the medians, Q1, Q3, interquartile

152
00:09:02.136 --> 00:09:07.721
ranges and whiskers are identical.
Yet series A

153
00:09:07.801 --> 00:09:11.333
contains five values and series
B contains a hundred.

154
00:09:11.809 --> 00:09:14.038
That is one limitation of this summary.

155
00:09:14.118 --> 00:09:16.295
Two distributions can have

156
00:09:16.375 --> 00:09:18.866
different shapes but similar quartiles.

157
00:09:18.946 --> 00:09:21.228
If we are interested in the full shape,

158
00:09:21.308 --> 00:09:24.642
we can complement
the box with a histogram.

159
00:09:24.722 --> 00:09:27.319
For example, in this case,

160
00:09:27.399 --> 00:09:29.260
the patterns are very different.

161
00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:32.616
Series A is very uniform, whereas

162
00:09:32.696 --> 00:09:35.135
series B contains subgroups,

163
00:09:35.215 --> 00:09:38.469
with clusters of values near Q1 and Q3.

164
00:09:38.561 --> 00:09:40.953
Yet the boxplots are identical.

165
00:09:41.033 --> 00:09:44.837
So we can complement
the box with a histogram.

166
00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:48.721
Another useful representation is a violin

167
00:09:48.801 --> 00:09:52.837
plot,
which shows the density of the points.

168
00:09:53.305 --> 00:09:57.705
The wider regions correspond
to the most common values.

169
00:09:57.785 --> 00:10:01.164
This can reveal several peaks

170
00:10:01.244 --> 00:10:04.987
that the boxplot did not necessarily show.

171
00:10:05.467 --> 00:10:07.565
Be careful: as with a histogram, the shape

172
00:10:07.645 --> 00:10:10.613
of a violin depends on a visualisation
choice, here smoothing.

173
00:10:10.693 --> 00:10:13.444
It can suggest values in places

174
00:10:13.524 --> 00:10:16.438
where no observations were recorded,

175
00:10:16.518 --> 00:10:18.344
especially with a small sample.

176
00:10:18.424 --> 00:10:21.392
Here, the bandwidth gradually increases

177
00:10:21.472 --> 00:10:24.713
and the violin becomes smoother.

178
00:10:24.793 --> 00:10:26.618
So, just as with a histogram,

179
00:10:26.698 --> 00:10:29.232
we need to choose the bandwidth carefully.

180
00:10:29.312 --> 00:10:32.959
Too much detail makes
the graph harder to read.

181
00:10:33.051 --> 00:10:35.510
Conversely, excessive smoothing,

182
00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.405
as shown here, removes details and makes

183
00:10:38.485 --> 00:10:41.147
the violin less useful.

184
00:10:41.227 --> 00:10:43.991
The outline affects how we read the plot,

185
00:10:44.071 --> 00:10:47.647
so we should always bear this in mind.

186
00:10:47.727 --> 00:10:51.050
Let us now compare
the violin and the boxplot.

187
00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.792
The boxplot provides

188
00:10:53.872 --> 00:10:56.535
more precise numerical reference points.

189
00:10:56.615 --> 00:10:59.103
We can see the median, Q1 and Q3.

190
00:10:59.227 --> 00:11:03.048
The violin shows the overall
density of the data.

191
00:11:03.128 --> 00:11:06.461
They complement each other:
we do not necessarily have to choose.

192
00:11:06.541 --> 00:11:08.460
We can even combine them

193
00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.557
in a single graph, as shown here.

194
00:11:10.637 --> 00:11:13.629
I have added the boxplot,
the violin and the points.

195
00:11:13.709 --> 00:11:16.896
You can see that the graph
is becoming crowded.

196
00:11:16.976 --> 00:11:19.431
This can be

197
00:11:19.511 --> 00:11:22.047
useful during exploratory analysis.

198
00:11:22.139 --> 00:11:25.286
But in a report

199
00:11:25.366 --> 00:11:28.967
or presentation, an overloaded graph

200
00:11:29.047 --> 00:11:32.727
may become hard to read.

201
00:11:33.275 --> 00:11:36.950
The box describes the observations well.

202
00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:40.335
However,
it does not give a margin of error,

203
00:11:40.415 --> 00:11:43.985
a confidence interval
or the uncertainty in the mean.

204
00:11:44.065 --> 00:11:46.947
I want to stress this point:

205
00:11:47.027 --> 00:11:50.667
the whiskers do not represent
these uncertainties.

206
00:11:50.747 --> 00:11:52.612
Confusion is common because they

207
00:11:52.692 --> 00:11:55.587
resemble error bars in other graphs.

208
00:11:55.667 --> 00:12:00.359
Here, the whiskers represent
the spread of the data.

209
00:12:01.153 --> 00:12:05.277
Now for a few points about readability.

210
00:12:05.357 --> 00:12:09.033
Here, we compare the North
and South catchments.

211
00:12:09.113 --> 00:12:12.508
North appears much more spread out.

212
00:12:12.588 --> 00:12:16.768
But look at the x-axis:
on the left, it runs

213
00:12:16.848 --> 00:12:21.445
from 2 to 3.5 mg/L,
while on the right it runs from 3 to 9.

214
00:12:21.537 --> 00:12:23.784
The axes are different,

215
00:12:23.864 --> 00:12:26.990
making comparison difficult.

216
00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:29.782
We therefore need a common scale

217
00:12:29.862 --> 00:12:32.574
to compare the catchments.

218
00:12:32.654 --> 00:12:34.695
Also add the sample size

219
00:12:34.775 --> 00:12:36.866
so that the graph can
be interpreted properly.

220
00:12:36.946 --> 00:12:39.038
Remember: the boxplot

221
00:12:39.118 --> 00:12:41.445
alone does not provide this information.

222
00:12:43.681 --> 00:12:47.811
For a study over time, the box has

223
00:12:47.891 --> 00:12:52.135
another limitation: it does not tell us

224
00:12:52.215 --> 00:12:56.515
when each measurement was taken.

225
00:12:56.595 --> 00:13:00.384
We can instead plot a time series. Here,

226
00:13:00.464 --> 00:13:04.389
North has twelve measurements,
one per month.

227
00:13:04.481 --> 00:13:06.862
We connect them with lines.

228
00:13:06.942 --> 00:13:10.435
Boxplots and violins
share this limitation:

229
00:13:10.515 --> 00:13:15.261
they do not show the time order.

230
00:13:15.905 --> 00:13:20.761
To sum up, a boxplot highlights extreme

231
00:13:20.841 --> 00:13:23.353
values,
especially those beyond the whiskers.

232
00:13:23.433 --> 00:13:25.388
The median is marked within the box.

233
00:13:25.468 --> 00:13:31.676
The interval from Q1 to Q3 contains
the central half of the data,

234
00:13:31.756 --> 00:13:35.125
and the whiskers help us

235
00:13:35.205 --> 00:13:38.289
identify observations that
deserve closer examination.

236
00:13:38.369 --> 00:13:41.754
To visualise observations, we use

237
00:13:41.834 --> 00:13:45.218
points, shapes and sometimes

238
00:13:45.298 --> 00:13:48.834
different colours. A boxplot helps

239
00:13:48.914 --> 00:13:52.449
us spot unusual values.

240
00:13:52.529 --> 00:13:55.931
Together with a violin, it also
gives a quick distribution summary.

241
00:13:56.011 --> 00:13:59.873
Always check sample sizes, missing data,

242
00:13:59.953 --> 00:14:04.970
units and common scales.

243
00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:07.888
Different axes can also

244
00:14:07.968 --> 00:14:09.989
make comparisons misleading.

245
00:14:10.433 --> 00:14:14.138
In the next video, we will explore

246
00:14:14.218 --> 00:14:18.760
other graphs for examining
relationships between

247
00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:22.869
variables and communicating results.
Boxplots

248
00:14:22.949 --> 00:14:26.600
and violins are powerful tools,

249
00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:29.627
but they are not enough on their own.
